  "content": {
    "title": "Social media criticizes referees for UConn Michigan foul disparity",
    "text": "UConn coach Dan Hurley reacts during the first half of a game against Michigan in the National Championship of the 2026 NCAA Men's Basketball Tournament at Lucas Oil Stadium on April 6, 2026 in Indianapolis, Indiana. Michael Reaves/Getty ImagesThe University of Michigan defeated UConn men's basketball 69-63 in a hard-fought national championship game in Indianapolis on Monday night. On social media, many observers questioned the foul disparity between the two teams, which they blame on poor officiating.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢In the game, the Huskies were called for 22 fouls, while the Wolverines were whistled for 13. Throughout much of the second half, UConn's foul total was more than doubled those called on Michigan..Several key UConn players, including Silas Demary Jr. (5), Solo Ball (4) and Tarris Reed Jr. (3), were charged with at least three fouls. Demary Jr., who was in foul trouble throughout the night and only played 21 minutes, fouled out with just over a minute left in the contest. Ball saw the floor for a scant 16 minutes.Michigan finished 25 of 28 from the free-throw line, while UConn shot 12 of 16.Make CT Insider a preferred source on Google to see more of our journalism when you search.Add Preferred SourceThe gap was not entirely unexpected. UConn's foul disparity across the season was larger than almost any other major conference team, and fouls plagued them during a loss to St. John's in the Big East tournament last month.Article continues below this ad Over the course of the season, Michigan was 18th in the nation in opponent free-throw rate. UConn was 303rd.James Breeding, one of the referees calling Monday night's championship game, has a long and fraught history with both Dan Hurley and the Huskies, which some online were quick to reference.On social media, UConn fans, family and even a Connecticut senator criticized the refereeing. The refs have taken control of this game. Fouls are 11 to 4? Ridiculous. Only reason Michigan is in this game.— Chris Murphy 🟧 (@ChrisMurphyCT) April 7, 2026 \"The refs have taken control of this game. Fouls are 11 to 4? Ridiculous. Only reason Michigan is in this game,\" Sen. Chris Murphy wrote on X.Article continues below this adMurphy's post led to a bit of intra-party disagreement, as Abdul El-Sayed, a Democratic candidate for senator in Michigan, fired back at the Connecticut senator's complaint. \"Stop complaining. Go Blue!\" he wrote.Demary Jr.'s father, Silas Demary Sr., agreed with the senator, though he used more colorful language.Article continues below this ad This shit is not real! UConn 13Michigan 4 foul count— Silas Demary Sr (DEM-uh-REE) (@sdemary) April 7, 2026 Former NFL quarterback and ESPN commentator Dan Orlovsky, a UConn alum, described the officiating as \"very poor\" in a post on X. Political journalist and MS Now contributor Sam Stein wrote that the refs were \"doing (Michigan's) bidding.\" The fouls are like 15-5 right now.It’s impossible to be this 1-sided call wise.The officiating is very poor.— Dan Orlovsky (@danorlovsky7) April 7, 2026 Respectfully. It's tough to beat Michigan normally. But the refs doing their bidding is nuts— Sam Stein (@samstein) April 7, 2026 NFL Redzone host Scott Hanson, no stranger to watching refereeing, also had harsh words for the officials.Article continues below this ad Officials having a hard time in the Natty— Scott Hanson (@ScottHanson) April 7, 2026 Many fans got in on the fun, with a variety of cracks at the refs, including posting photos of them in the distinctive maize and gold colors of Michigan. Bill Murray just appeared on the Jumbotron in a UConn shirt and got called for a foul.— Brandon Walker (@BFW) April 7, 2026 Score exclusive insights and analysis on UConn athletics.April 6, 2026|Updated April 7, 2026 12:13 a.m.Nathaniel Rosenberg is a Hearst Fellow covering breaking and trending news with Hearst Connecticut. He previously wrote for the Philadelphia Inquirer, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel and New Haven Independent. Nathaniel graduated from Yale University with a degree in History. Please send him tips, story ideas and any amusing jokes at nathaniel.rosenberg@hearst.com.",
